{"t":1793213572,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"0bb31f19-7572-4886-baef-0fc8465c4b93","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-28T14:03:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 281403\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n1003 AM EDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\n\nRemoved fog along/east of I-95 this morning as the cloud bank \ndid not reach that far east. Made minor changes to aviation and \nmarine; see appropriate sections below for additional details. \nOtherwise, no major changes made to the forecast this morning. \n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Low clouds and fog through Thursday morning. \n \n- 2) Well above normal temperatures Thursday and Friday\n  afternoons, then more seasonable Saturday.\n\n- 3) On-and-off rain showers are possible beginning Sunday into\n  early next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Low clouds and fog through Thursday morning. \n\nOnshore flow developing around high pressure to the north and\neast, as well as a weak warm front slowly lifting across the\nMid-Atlantic, will set the stage for increasing low-level\nmoisture. This results in the development of low clouds and fog\nfor much of the area, persisting into Thursday morning.\n\nFor today, low clouds likely linger through midday into early \nafternoon closer to the eastern (upslope) areas between the Blue\nRidge/Catoctin Mountains and US-15.\n\nWarm advection mid-level clouds may then persist much of the \nday as the weak warm front lifts north and moistening onshore \nflow continues. This is expected to keep temperatures a few\ndegrees cooler this afternoon when compared to the previous\ncouple of afternoons (i.e. upper 60s instead of lower 70s).\n\nLow clouds and some fog redevelop by late evening, persisting \ninto Thursday morning. Given a slightly more southeast (versus \neast) component to the low-level wind, this may allow low \nclouds/fog to expand further east to and across the I-95 \ncorridor. Southern Maryland to the Northern Neck of Virginia are\ncloser in proximity to the moisture sources of the Atlantic \nOcean, Chesapeake Bay and tidal Potomac River, and may therefore\nhave a chance at some drizzle by early Thursday.\n\nMore appreciable clearing is expected by mid/late Thursday \nmorning as winds shift to a bit west of south, helping to scour \nout some of the low-level moisture.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Well above normal temperatures Thursday and Friday\nafternoons, then more seasonable Saturday.\n\nFollowing a wind shift to the south and southwest on Thursday,\nwell above normal temperatures are expected. Afternoon high \ntemperatures reach into the lower to middle 70s. A dry front\nlooks to cross the area Friday turning winds to the northwest,\nthough I hesitate to call this a cold front with the lack of\ncold air advection in its wake. The airmass will be drier, and\nthat coupled with downsloping flow is expected to push high\ntemperatures well into the middle to upper 70s on Friday. While\nthis is a solid 8 to 15 degrees above normal for late October, \nit is still several degrees shy of daily records.\n\nSome cold advection does take hold Friday night in the wake of a\nsecondary boundary as winds shift to the north. This should\nallow for low temperatures to fall into the upper 30s and 40s.\n\nA combination of subtle cold air advection and increased clouds\nahead of troughing developing over the middle of the country \nwill result in more seasonable daytime high temperatures on \nSaturday - generally in the 60s (50s mountains). Anticipate lows\ndropping into the 40s Saturday night with a continued increase \nin clouds but near zero chance of precipitation.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...On-and-off rain showers are possible beginning \nSunday into early next week.\n\nA broad area of low pressure will form over the Midwest, and \nthen track east late in the weekend. There is a potential for \nrain showers beginning on Sunday, though it has trended drier \nover the past several model runs. Mentionable precipitation \nchances now hold off until Sunday evening in the latest forecast\npackage. These chances linger into Monday. Temperatures remain \nin the upper 60s for highs Sunday and Monday (50s mountains), \nnear to slightly above average for the start of November.\n\nAs the initial system tracks offshore by Tuesday, a second, \npotentially more potent upper-level low could form over the \nPlains and make its way to the region by the middle of next \nweek. Uncertainty remains high regarding the track of this \nsystem, but this could bring additional rainfall to the region.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /13Z WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\n\nA bank of clouds east of the Blue Ridge persists into late this\nmorning, but will gradually clear out throughout the day. \nConditions eventually lift to VFR by this afternoon.\n\nAdditional low clouds or fog are expected to develop late this\nevening into the overnight, with higher potential reaching\nfurther east into the I-95 corridor TAF sites as light east\nwinds shift to southeast. Clouds should lift mid to late morning\nThursday as winds shift to the south and southwest in the wake\nof a lifting warm front, with VFR then likely through Friday as\na dry cold front approaches/crosses. Winds will shift to the\nnorthwest Friday, north Friday night, then northeast to east on\nSaturday as another area of high pressure builds to the north.\n\nSunday has been trending drier as high pressure persists, \nalthough there is increasing uncertainty at this range of the \nforecast. Light southerly winds are expected during this time.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\n\nLight east to southeast flow is expected through tonight. Winds\nshift to the south and southwest Thursday, then become \nnorthwest to north Friday into Friday night. There may be an \nincrease in winds due to increased vertical mixing over near- \nshore/narrower waterways Friday afternoon, with northerly \nchanneling more likely to bring gusts closer to SCA thresholds \nFriday night.\n\nWinds then remain below SCA thresholds through the weekend. \nWinds become northeast to east Saturday, then southerly Sunday.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nLight onshore flow through Thursday may push sensitive \nshoreline close to minor flood stage. Areas most at risk for \nreaching minor tidal flood thresholds would be places like \nAnnapolis and Alexandria mainly during the evening tide cycles \n(which are astronomically higher).\n\nLight offshore flow should lower tidal anomalies Friday into\nSaturday, before light southerly flow possibly causes at least a\nsmall rebound on Sunday.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...DHOF/CPB/SRT\nAVIATION...DHOF/CPB/SRT\nMARINE...DHOF/CPB/SRT\n"},"forecast":{"t":1793213470,"days":{"2026-10-28":{"hi":69,"lo":50,"pop":1,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-10-29":{"hi":73,"lo":52,"pop":1,"short":"Areas Of Fog then Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-30":{"hi":75,"lo":46,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-31":{"hi":65,"lo":47,"pop":1,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-01":{"hi":68,"lo":49,"pop":7,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-11-02":{"hi":69,"lo":52,"pop":14,"short":"Mostly Cloudy"},"2026-11-03":{"hi":70,"lo":53,"pop":17,"short":"Slight Chance Rain Showers"}}},"grid":{"t0":1793210400,"n":48,"ceil":[24900,24800,24800,24700,23900,24200,21800,22300,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,10100,8800,9500,10100,10800,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,6.0,10.0,10.0,9.4,9.4,7.7,7.7,7.5,7.5,8.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0],"spd":[4,4,3,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,1,1,1,0,0,0,0,0,